{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Reuben, Simeon, etc.</b> He enumerated their names here, after the manner of the midrash (Leviticus Rabbah 32:5) — for four things were our forefathers redeemed from Egypt: in the merit that they did not change their name, and did not change their tongue, and that they were fenced off from forbidden relations, and that there were no informers among them, etc. And the reason for this is as he concludes in the Yalkut on this portion, that all the names of the tribes were called after the redemption: Reuben — ״רָאֹה רָאִיתִי אֶת עֳנִי עַמִּי״ (Exodus 3:7). Simeon — after ״וַיִּשְׁמַע אֱלֹהִים אֶת נַאֲקָתָם״ (Exodus 2:24), and so for all of them. And according to this, if they had changed their name, it would have had no impression upon the redemption and they would not have been worthy of being redeemed, since they exchanged their names which indicated the redemption, calling themselves by a new name that bears no indication of the redemption. Or the change of name would have been a cause for despairing of the redemption, and they would not have sought the face of HaShem for the redemption and for the exchange." "And likewise, if they had changed their tongue, even though they had not changed their name, even so their name would not have indicated the redemption, for specifically in the holy tongue does ״רְאוּבֵן״ indicate after ״רָאֹה רָאִיתִי עֳנִי עַמִּי״ (Exodus 3:7), and ״שִׁמְעוֹן״ after ״וַיִּשְׁמַע אֱלֹהִים״ (Exodus 2:24), but in another tongue the name ״רְאוּבֵן״ does not indicate ״רָאֹה רָאִיתִי״, and so for all of them. And in the merit that they were fenced off from forbidden relations, therefore they were not worthy of being among the Egyptians, who are steeped in lust, concerning whom it is said of them (Ezekiel 23:20) ״וְזִרְמַת סוּסִים זִרְמָתָם״. And because there was no evil tongue among them, and it was appointed concerning them ״וְשָׁאֲלוּ אִשָּׁה מֵאֵת רְעוּתָהּ״ (Exodus 3:22), therefore they were redeemed — for were the matter not appointed, they would not have lent them anything, and then it would have been impossible to redeem them, so that one should not say that old man — ״וַעֲבָדוּם וְעִנּוּ אֹתָם״ (Genesis 15:13) was fulfilled in them, but ״וְאַחֲרֵי כֵן יֵצְאוּ בִּרְכֻשׁ גָּדוֹל״ (Genesis 15:14) was not fulfilled in them. Therefore it says ״וְאֵלֶּה שְׁמוֹת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, for even though Joseph died and the enslavement was about to come, even so the impress of the redemption remained in force, for these names of theirs came with them when they came to be enslaved, and with them are they called even now, for their names were not changed. And this is proof that their tongue too they did not change, for if not so, there is no practical consequence in the fact that they did not change their names." "<b>Each man and his household came.</b> That they were fenced off from forbidden relations, for each one cleaved to his wife and not to another. And further, that they took wives for themselves before they came to Egypt, so that they would not marry women from the Egyptians, who are steeped in lust. And afterward he enumerated Reuben, Simeon, etc., for these names indicate the redemption."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:49.141419758Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}